WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:10.338
In this video, you will add an event to the map, like a tree lighting, a market, a parade, or a haunted house, so families across Southern California can find it.

00:00:10.338 --> 00:00:11.557
We start on the map.

00:00:12.458 --> 00:00:16.520
To begin, open your web browser, and go to SoCal Lights dot app.

00:00:16.520 --> 00:00:18.645
That is the only address you need.

00:00:20.789 --> 00:00:23.196
This is the SoCal Lights home page.

00:00:23.196 --> 00:00:26.940
The first time you visit, it asks to use your location.

00:00:26.940 --> 00:00:28.277
Tap Use my location.

00:00:30.202 --> 00:00:34.639
That is how the map shows how far things are, and gives you directions.

00:00:34.639 --> 00:00:37.844
Your browser asks permission as well, so tap Allow.

00:00:37.844 --> 00:00:42.404
Your location is used only on your device, and you can say no at any time.

00:00:46.303 --> 00:00:48.993
Now look at the top right corner of the map.

00:00:48.993 --> 00:00:50.188
See the three dots?

00:00:50.188 --> 00:00:54.491
That is the menu, and it is the doorway to everything else on the site.

00:00:54.491 --> 00:00:55.029
Tap them.

00:00:56.636 --> 00:00:57.899
In the menu, tap Add.

00:00:57.899 --> 00:01:02.204
That is where you add anything new: an event, a contest, or your own house.

00:01:03.932 --> 00:01:05.065
Now tap An event.

00:01:05.065 --> 00:01:12.806
Events are for things the whole community can attend, like a tree lighting, a holiday parade, a market, or a haunted house.

00:01:14.678 --> 00:01:18.020
The first question is whether your event has a web page.

00:01:18.020 --> 00:01:20.131
If it does, paste the address here.

00:01:20.131 --> 00:01:27.108
When the page mentions your event's name and date, your event is marked Confirmed on the map, which people trust more.

00:01:27.108 --> 00:01:31.446
If you do not have one, tap No, Next, and the event still goes on the map.

00:01:33.241 --> 00:01:35.964
Type the event's name, the way people know it.

00:01:35.964 --> 00:01:39.498
Then choose the season it belongs to, and the type of event.

00:01:39.498 --> 00:01:42.627
That is how visitors find it when they filter the map.

00:01:45.251 --> 00:01:47.024
Pick the first and last day.

00:01:47.024 --> 00:01:49.836
For a one-day event, pick the same day twice.

00:01:49.836 --> 00:01:50.691
Then tap Next.

00:01:52.655 --> 00:01:54.448
Is it free to attend, or paid?

00:01:54.448 --> 00:01:57.108
Families plan around this, so say it clearly.

00:01:57.108 --> 00:01:58.265
Tap Free, then Next.

00:02:00.830 --> 00:02:01.448
Now where.

00:02:01.448 --> 00:02:04.932
Type the name of the place, the city, and the street address.

00:02:04.932 --> 00:02:10.046
The street address puts a pin in the right spot on the map, so families can get directions.

00:02:12.998 --> 00:02:14.976
Describe it in a sentence or two.

00:02:14.976 --> 00:02:16.780
What will families see and do?

00:02:16.780 --> 00:02:20.038
That is the text people read before they decide to come.

00:02:23.596 --> 00:02:25.079
Last, check everything.

00:02:25.079 --> 00:02:27.799
You can change any line with its Edit link.

00:02:27.799 --> 00:02:30.642
Nothing is public until you tap Add my event.

00:02:30.642 --> 00:02:32.620
Your event then goes on the map.

00:02:34.666 --> 00:02:36.351
Next up: Show off your house.
